# Unique bounded measure realizing the critical polynomial moments

Theorem statement: `MTT.measure_extension` (`ede62bad-efd1-46eb-b41d-ba9cf5f2a802`), by
davidloeffler, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `68b5a2e8-d2ad-4ea7-9d7f-6be6321f5bed`, by davidloeffler, 2026-09-06 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

For each sign and each ordinary root, the prescribed disk moments extend to exactly one continuous
Cp-linear functional on C(Zp*,Cp). On every unit residue disk of positive depth, this measure
integrates X^j to the prescribed moment for every 0 ≤ j ≤ k−2. The assertion also supplies the
continuous disk test functions pointwise, rather than assuming their existence.

## Explanation of the source proof

For an ordinary eigenform with a signed period system and unit root, we reduce the existence and
uniqueness statement
$$\exists!\,\mu_s:\quad \int_{a+p^n\mathbf Z_p}x^j\,d\mu_s(x)=M_{s,j}(n,a),\qquad 0\le j\le k-2,$$
to the order-zero polynomial-moment extension criterion of Colmez, the MTT distribution relation,
and the ordinary centred-moment bound. These three imported results are open dependencies of this
reduction.

The general criterion is formulated for arbitrary moments, without any modular-form data. It takes a
finite degree bound, representative-independent degree-zero masses, subdivision compatibility in
every degree, and one uniform bound for the centred moments. Its conclusion gives a unique bounded
measure realizing all the specified polynomial moments. It is the $r=0$ specialization of the
extension theorem in Colmez, *Fonctions d'une variable p-adique*, author PDF §II.3, Théorème
II.3.2(ii), pp. 32–33, on the compact open unit group. The higher-moment conclusion comes from the
vanishing argument at the end of that proof; no separate MTT-specific approximation theorem is
assumed.

**Representative independence.** This part is proved directly in the submission. The cusp form has
period one, so its degree-zero modular integral is invariant under integer translation of the
rational cusp. The same holds for the signed integral, since reflection replaces the translation
integer by its negative. The period-comparison identity and injectivity of the complex embedding
therefore give
$$P_s(0,r+z)=P_s(0,r)\qquad(z\in\mathbf Z).$$
If two integer centres are congruent modulo $p^n$, their associated rational cusps at depths $n$ and
$n-1$ differ by integers. Degree-zero algebraic symbols are exactly these normalized signed values.
Both terms in the definition of the disk mass consequently agree. This proof uses neither the
distribution relation nor the centred bound.

**Apply the general theorem.** Fix the sign and set the degree bound to $k-2$, with
$$M_j(n,a)=M_{s,j}(n,a).$$
The MTT distribution relation supplies the subdivision identity in every critical degree. The
strengthened ordinary bound supplies a constant, independent of centre, depth and degree, satisfying
$$\left|\sum_{t=0}^j\binom jt(-a)^{j-t}M_t(n,a)\right|_p\le C p^{-nj}.$$
Together with representative independence, these are precisely the general extension theorem's
hypotheses. Its conclusion is the mission's `RealizesMoments` predicate after unfolding the disk
test function, coordinate and measure abbreviations. Existence and uniqueness therefore transfer
directly to the target.

The older degree-zero-only bound and the proposed separate polynomial-moment compatibility lemma are
not dependencies of this reduction.

